@startuml F01-attachment-scale
!theme plain
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFEBEE
skinparam defaultFontSize 11

title F-01 — Attachment Scale Bomb\nNo size limit, no offloading strategy; Philippines-scale calculations

rectangle "Per Referral" #FFF9C4 {
  note as perref
    ServiceRequest.supportingInfo → 0..* clinical resources
    Each resource may embed Attachment data (Binary/base64)
    Example: lab result PDFs, imaging reports, referral slip scans

    Realistic clinical summary for 1 referral:
      • Chief complaint narrative
      • 1-2 lab result PDFs        (~500 KB each)
      • 1 imaging report PDF       (~500 KB)
      • Referral slip scan         (~300 KB)
      • Medication list            (~50 KB text)

    Plausible per-referral payload: 1–2 MB inline FHIR content
    IG cap stated: none (FHIR base has no normative size limit)
  end note
}

rectangle "Per Facility Per Day" #FFE0CC {
  note as perfac
    Conservative: rural health unit (RHU)
      10–20 referrals/day × 1.5 MB = 15–30 MB/day
    Moderate: district hospital outpatient
      40–80 referrals/day × 1.5 MB = 60–120 MB/day
    High: city hospital referral hub
      100–200 referrals/day × 1.5 MB = 150–300 MB/day
  end note
}

rectangle "Philippines National Scale" #FFCCBC {
  note as national
    Public health facilities (DOH estimate):
      ~21,000 Barangay Health Stations (BHS)
      ~2,600  Rural Health Units (RHUs)
      ~1,200  District/City/Provincial hospitals
      ~700    Tertiary/Specialist hospitals
    Total: ~25,500 public facilities

    Plus ~9,000 PhilHealth-accredited private facilities
    Conservative active eReferral facilities (early rollout): ~10,000

    Low estimate (10,000 facilities × 20 MB/day):
      200 GB/day ingest
      73 TB/year

    Full rollout (25,000 facilities × 50 MB/day):
      1.25 TB/day ingest
      456 TB/year

    At 40 referrals/day/facility × 500 KB each × 25,000 facilities:
      500 GB/day
      182 TB/year
  end note
}

rectangle "What the IG Does NOT Provide" #FFAAAA {
  note as missing
    ❌ No attachment size limit (no profile constraint)
    ❌ No IHE MHD DocumentReference offloading pattern
    ❌ No Binary resource with external URL strategy
    ❌ No CDN / blob storage reference (S3, Azure Blob, GCS)
    ❌ No SMART on FHIR bulk data export for attachments
    ❌ No server capability statement requirement for max attachment size
    ❌ No deduplication strategy (same lab result referenced in N referrals)
    ❌ No retention / deletion policy for attachments
    ❌ No compression requirement

    Every attachment is base64-encoded inline in the FHIR resource.
    A single FHIR transaction bundle with 5 PDF attachments
    could be 3–5 MB of JSON.
  end note
}

rectangle "Attack / Stress Vector" #FF8A80 {
  note as attack
    A single malformed POST:
      ServiceRequest with supportingInfo containing:
        40 × DocumentReference each with 1 MB attachment
      = 40 MB in one FHIR transaction

    A FHIR server with no input size limit:
      → accepts 40 MB JSON body
      → stores 40 MB of base64 content in the database
      → indexes all references
      → returns a 200 OK

    HAPI FHIR default max POST body: server configuration
    The IG does not specify a server-side limit.
    The CapabilityStatement does not declare maxRequestBodySize.

    Repeated 1000 times from one IP address:
      40 GB of FHIR attachment data ingested
      Server storage exhausted or severely degraded
      (unintentional DoS from misconfigured real system
       or intentional from adversary)
  end note
}

@enduml
